Expected Outcomes
Every row in the RBAC test matrix reports PASS, confirming that role boundaries are enforced at the Argo CD policy engine level with no privilege bleed between roles.
The Argo CD server debug logs contain a structured JSON entry for each denied action, and argocd account can-i decisions are consistent with those log entries for all three users.
Troubleshooting
If argocd account can-i returns yes for an action you expected to be denied, what sequence of policy rules â including the policy.default fallback and any group inheritance chains â could produce an unintended allow decision, and how would you isolate which specific rule is responsible?

If the Argo CD server logs show no RBAC enforcement entries after triggering a denied action, what configuration gap in argocd-cmd-params-cm or the server restart sequence could explain the absence, and what would you check first to confirm the new log level is actually active?

Conclusion
This lab demonstrated how Argo CD's Casbin-based RBAC engine enforces access boundaries across users, AppProjects, and application objects through a declarative policy ConfigMap. You built a multi-tier role hierarchy, scoped permissions to individual projects, and validated every boundary programmatically using both the can-i API and server-side audit logs. These patterns transfer directly to production environments where separation of duties between development, operations, and administrative personas is a compliance requirement.
